          "overview": "Session d02 (9 turns) discussed only paper 2004.00809, \"Mapping Languages and Demographics with Georeferenced Corpora\"—the first and last paper cited. The recurring question was whether the authors offer a hypothesis for why Twitter data better predicts each country's language inventory. Early turns recorded the result (fewer missing languages but more false positives), regional patterns (better in North America and Oceania), the English/Spanish frequency confound, wealth (GDP-weighted) and urban-collection explanations, and the absence of a dedicated tested causal hypothesis; later turns split answerable from unanswerable points and added baseline goals, locals-vs-visitors for over-represented African countries, fixed-threshold bias, and limited census coverage. Unresolved: whether general representativeness explanations count as a hypothesis for the inventory advantage; sources of false positives (migration, tourism, business); relative weight of mechanisms; whether rural-centered collection would bias similarly. No other unresolved references.",
